James Rising inquired about imputing missing \(R^2\) values in regression models, seeking references for best practices. I highlighted the limited literature on imputing summary statistics like \(R^2\) and recommended focusing on general missing data and multiple imputation (MI) theories. I provided key references, including Rubin (1987) for MI fundamentals, van Buuren (2018) for practical guidance, and others related to meta-analysis and handling derived statistics. I emphasized the importance of imputing underlying variables (when possible), conducting multiple imputations, and performing sensitivity analyses. I cautioned against directly imputing \(R^2\) without strong auxiliary variables and offered to provide a curated bibliography, a methods-paragraph template, or R imputation strategies tailored to specific \(R^2\) types.
